    "body": "AngloGold Ashanti plans to spend $2.5 billion to build its Quebradona copper project in Colombia. Credit: AngloGold Ashanti\nPosted By: Henry Lazenby November 18, 2026\nAngloGold Ashanti (NYSE: AU; JSE: ANG) plans to spend $2.5 billion (C$3.6 billion) to build its Quebradona copper project in Colombia if it secures an environmental licence that has eluded the company since its first application in 2019.\nThe underground project, about 60 km southwest of Antioquia’s capital, Medellín, could produce about 80,000 tonnes of copper a year and generate about $1.5 billion in annual exports, AngloGold said Tuesday at an investment event in New York.\n“We are confident that, once the environmental approvals are obtained, the project will move towards the start of construction in 2028,” Colombian Vice-President José Manuel Restrepo said in a statement issued in Spanish by AngloGold’s local unit.\nThat timetable hinges in part on a 2025 order that created a temporary protected area covering the project and bars new environmental licences for mining in the zone while it’s in force. The restriction is due to run into mid-2028. AngloGold challenged the order in court and wrote down $98 million in project costs that year.\nAngloGold shares fell 7.9% to $78.01 Wednesday afternoon in New York amid global declines in mining and metals stocks. That valued the company at about $39.5 billion.\nPermitting question\nAngloGold CEO Alberto Calderón told Colombia’s Blu Radio on Wednesday that he expects the licensing process to take 12 to 18 months. AngloGold could spend about $2 billion in the first two years of construction if it secures the licence, he said.\nColombia’s environmental licensing agency shelved the application in 2021 after identifying gaps in the company’s studies, including on groundwater, tailings storage and potential ground subsidence. The National Mining Agency in August extended exploration to December 2027, giving AngloGold time to complete more geological, environmental and water studies. The company has begun consulting communities for a new environmental impact study.\nQuebradona hosts 120 million probable tonnes grading 1.23% copper for 1.47 million tonnes of contained metal, according to AngloGold’s year-end 2025 estimate. The planned plant would produce a copper concentrate with gold and silver as byproducts.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: South America\nCOMPANY: AngloGold Ashanti\nRepublish this article",
